WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.893
This briefing comes from
Book 04 of the Federal

3
00:00:03.893 --> 00:00:07.025
Modernization Workbook,
Access and Privilege.

4
00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:12.600
Book 02 moved the identity, and
Book 03 made its facts trustworthy.

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.524
This book is about what happens
with those facts on every sign-in:

6
00:00:16.524 --> 00:00:19.412
who gets access to what,
under which conditions,

7
00:00:19.412 --> 00:00:23.262
and how administrator power is
kept from becoming an open door.

8
00:00:23.262 --> 00:00:25.800
We'll take it through
why, what, who and how.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:31.212
And the short version is this: in Zero
Trust, every access decision is a policy

10
00:00:31.212 --> 00:00:36.538
decision, and the policy library is, in
practice, the agency's access control.

11
00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:38.150
Let's start with why.

12
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:45.050
Why is every access
decision a policy decision?

13
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:49.688
Because federal Zero Trust policy says
so, and the platform works that way.

14
00:00:49.688 --> 00:00:56.313
M-22-09 requires every request to be evaluated,
with no trust from network location.

15
00:00:56.313 --> 00:01:01.362
NIST describes a policy decision point,
separate from where access is enforced,

16
00:01:01.362 --> 00:01:03.963
and in Entra ID that's Conditional Access.

17
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:08.517
Its rules are simple: every policy
that applies must be satisfied,

18
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:11.975
a block wins, and exclusions
override everything.

19
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:15.900
What it replaces is access decided
once and trusted afterward.

20
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.100
Inside the network, so trusted.

21
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.738
In the group, so allowed, forever.

22
00:01:20.738 --> 00:01:23.322
Those assumptions survive
a migration unless

23
00:01:23.322 --> 00:01:26.038
someone deliberately
replaces them with policy.

24
00:01:26.038 --> 00:01:28.326
Two consequences run through the book.

25
00:01:28.326 --> 00:01:33.338
Every exclusion is a standing hole, so
each one needs an owner and a review.

26
00:01:33.338 --> 00:01:37.138
And policy is only as good
as the attributes it reads,

27
00:01:37.138 --> 00:01:39.890
which is why Book 03 matters here.

28
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.165
The same logic applies to administrators,
and this is where most breaches go.

29
00:01:48.165 --> 00:01:51.347
A standing administrator
is always on, at any hour,

30
00:01:51.347 --> 00:01:55.227
so any stolen credential that
reaches the role gets all of it.

31
00:01:55.227 --> 00:02:00.514
Stronger sign-in helps, but it doesn't
narrow what the role can do, where, or when.

32
00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:02.977
An eligible administrator is different.

33
00:02:02.977 --> 00:02:08.175
The role is switched on only when needed,
for a set time, approved by someone else,

34
00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:12.014
after a fresh strong sign-in,
and every elevation is logged.

35
00:02:12.014 --> 00:02:16.333
NSA and CISA rank poor separation
of admin privilege among the most

36
00:02:16.333 --> 00:02:20.878
common misconfigurations they find,
and NIST requires least privilege.

37
00:02:20.878 --> 00:02:25.859
There's one standing exception: two
break-glass accounts stay permanently active,

38
00:02:25.859 --> 00:02:29.228
so the tenant can be recovered
if everything else fails.

39
00:02:29.228 --> 00:02:32.722
They're safe only when
governed: phishing-resistant,

40
00:02:32.722 --> 00:02:35.403
monitored, and tested every ninety days.

41
00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:38.180
Next, what the policy engine looks at.

42
00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.642
So what does Conditional
Access actually evaluate?

43
00:02:44.642 --> 00:02:48.576
First, signals: who the user
is and what roles they hold,

44
00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:52.955
their device, the application,
the location, and sign-in risk.

45
00:02:52.955 --> 00:02:56.980
Conditions decide which policies
apply to this particular request.

46
00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:00.771
Then controls: block, or
allow with requirements,

47
00:03:00.771 --> 00:03:05.130
like MFA, a compliant device
or an app protection policy.

48
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.755
And session controls, such as
how often to sign in again.

49
00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.455
Three operating rules
run through the library.

50
00:03:11.455 --> 00:03:14.383
Every policy runs in
report-only mode first,

51
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:17.718
so you see its impact before
it enforces anything.

52
00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:20.961
Policies target personas,
like admins, staff,

53
00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:24.555
guests and workload
identities, not individuals.

54
00:03:24.555 --> 00:03:27.818
And deleted policies can be
recovered for thirty days.

55
00:03:27.818 --> 00:03:33.793
One recent change is worth knowing: the
approved client app grant went read-only

56
00:03:33.793 --> 00:03:39.400
on June 30, 2026, so mobile access
now relies on app protection policies.

57
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.100
Underneath sit two structures:
privilege and applications.

58
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.843
For privilege, use the
least-privileged role for each task,

59
00:03:50.843 --> 00:03:55.600
and keep fewer than five Global
Administrators, as Microsoft recommends.

60
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:00.442
Use role-assignable groups, and tier
everything along Microsoft's enterprise access

61
00:04:00.442 --> 00:04:05.012
model, which builds on the old Active
Directory tiers rather than replacing them.

62
00:04:05.012 --> 00:04:09.032
For applications, the surprise is
that app owners hold real admin

63
00:04:09.032 --> 00:04:13.275
rights over their app, including
adding a credential, so keep them few.

64
00:04:13.275 --> 00:04:16.133
Admin consent goes through
a governed workflow,

65
00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:21.350
and apps authenticate with certificates or
federated credentials instead of secrets.

66
00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.157
And here's what people get wrong:
consent grants never expire,

67
00:04:25.157 --> 00:04:28.887
and revoking one leaves tokens
already issued still valid.

68
00:04:28.887 --> 00:04:32.788
So application access needs the
same review discipline as people.

69
00:04:32.788 --> 00:04:34.740
Next, who decides.

70
00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.990
Who decides access,
and who carries it out?

71
00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.740
The book keeps those apart.

72
00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:47.673
Deciding: business and application owners
decide who should have access and why,

73
00:04:47.673 --> 00:04:50.986
the CISO owns the policy
baseline and its exceptions,

74
00:04:50.986 --> 00:04:54.815
and an identity governance office
owns approvals and reviews.

75
00:04:54.815 --> 00:04:58.857
Executing: identity engineering
builds the policies and settings,

76
00:04:58.857 --> 00:05:02.104
the help desk handles
assignments under a scoped role,

77
00:05:02.104 --> 00:05:05.352
and security operations
handles alerts and response.

78
00:05:05.352 --> 00:05:08.041
Assuring: the ISSO owns the evidence,

79
00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:13.865
the authorizing official owns accepted
risk, and reviewers recertify access.

80
00:05:13.865 --> 00:05:15.674
The rule that holds it together:

81
00:05:15.674 --> 00:05:19.015
the people who execute access
changes never approve them.

82
00:05:19.015 --> 00:05:23.065
That's why the help desk gets a custom
role that manages assignments only.

83
00:05:23.065 --> 00:05:27.696
The old guide gave it a role that
could grant consent and act as any app,

84
00:05:27.696 --> 00:05:30.240
so the rule existed only on paper.

85
00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:34.603
How.

86
00:05:34.603 --> 00:05:37.564
The policy library
rolls out in five waves.

87
00:05:37.564 --> 00:05:41.353
It starts with the foundation,
blocking legacy authentication.

88
00:05:41.353 --> 00:05:44.062
Then administrators,
with phishing-resistant

89
00:05:44.062 --> 00:05:46.702
sign-in from privileged
access workstations.

90
00:05:46.702 --> 00:05:51.502
Then everyone, with an MFA baseline
and device and app controls.

91
00:05:51.502 --> 00:05:54.977
Then risk, blocking
high-risk users and sign-ins.

92
00:05:54.977 --> 00:05:59.453
And finally the remaining segments,
like guests and workload identities.

93
00:05:59.453 --> 00:06:03.885
Every policy passes the same gates:
two to four weeks in report-only,

94
00:06:03.885 --> 00:06:08.077
a pilot ring, wider rings, and
then enforcement with monitoring.

95
00:06:08.077 --> 00:06:12.253
A wave only starts after the previous
one has run a week without incident.

96
00:06:12.253 --> 00:06:17.614
And rollback means switching a policy off
or back to report-only, never deleting it.

97
00:06:17.614 --> 00:06:20.340
The library is built to CISA's baseline.

98
00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.139
It keeps Book 02's policies
and adds new ones for the

99
00:06:24.139 --> 00:06:29.460
BOD 25-01 requirements; the
slide shows the numbering.

100
00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:35.780
For administrators, Privileged Identity

101
00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:39.010
Management turns privilege
into a governed request.

102
00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.960
An administrator holds an eligible
assignment, not an active one.

103
00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.475
To use it, they ask for activation
with a reason and a ticket,

104
00:06:46.475 --> 00:06:49.624
step up their sign-in, get
approval from someone else,

105
00:06:49.624 --> 00:06:53.798
and receive a time-boxed activation
that's logged and later reviewed.

106
00:06:53.798 --> 00:06:57.197
The details matter, and the
old guides had several wrong.

107
00:06:57.197 --> 00:07:00.111
Activation runs from one
to twenty-four hours.

108
00:07:00.111 --> 00:07:03.803
PIM's built-in MFA can be
satisfied by an earlier sign-in,

109
00:07:03.803 --> 00:07:08.235
so the book forces a fresh step-up
with an authentication context.

110
00:07:08.235 --> 00:07:10.810
Approvers can't approve
their own requests.

111
00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:14.960
And if the PIM licenses lapse,
eligible assignments are removed.

112
00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:20.530
Appendix B sets each role's activation
time, approvers and notifications,

113
00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.920
tighter for Tier 0 and Tier
1 than Book 02's first table.

114
00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:31.833
Finally, governance, code and evidence.

115
00:07:31.833 --> 00:07:36.931
Governance means access is requested
through packages with approval and an expiry

116
00:07:36.931 --> 00:07:39.189
date, reviews run on a tiered schedule,

117
00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:41.957
and guests have sponsors
and get reviewed too.

118
00:07:41.957 --> 00:07:44.026
As code means Conditional Access,

119
00:07:44.026 --> 00:07:47.570
PIM settings and access packages
live in version control.

120
00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.337
Changes go through review
instead of the portal,

121
00:07:50.337 --> 00:07:52.620
and drift is watched against a baseline.

122
00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.594
Evidence means all of this maps to the
federal access-control and monitoring

123
00:07:57.594 --> 00:08:02.645
controls, with every elevation and every
review on record; the slide lists them.

124
00:08:02.645 --> 00:08:04.683
And there are two traps in the tooling.

125
00:08:04.683 --> 00:08:09.086
Reviewing an application doesn't remove
members of the groups assigned to it,

126
00:08:09.086 --> 00:08:11.082
so the groups need their own review.

127
00:08:11.082 --> 00:08:14.877
And Entra keeps sign-in and
audit logs for only thirty days,

128
00:08:14.877 --> 00:08:18.240
so the evidence has to be
exported from day one.

129
00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.215
So, four asks.

130
00:08:23.215 --> 00:08:26.451
Adopt Book 04's Conditional Access library

131
00:08:26.451 --> 00:08:29.777
and wave plan as the
agency's access baseline.

132
00:08:29.777 --> 00:08:34.127
End standing privilege: every
administrator role eligible through PIM,

133
00:08:34.127 --> 00:08:38.403
with two governed break-glass accounts
as the only standing exception.

134
00:08:38.403 --> 00:08:41.600
Separate deciding from
executing: name the owners,

135
00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.939
scope the help desk role, and stand
up the identity governance office.

136
00:08:45.939 --> 00:08:49.079
And manage access as code,
with version control,

137
00:08:49.079 --> 00:08:53.265
review and drift monitoring,
keeping the evidence from day one.

138
00:08:53.265 --> 00:08:57.565
In Zero Trust, every access
decision is a policy decision.

139
00:08:57.565 --> 00:09:00.034
Writing that policy down, testing it,

140
00:09:00.034 --> 00:09:04.477
and proving it ran is how an agency
knows who can reach what, and why.

141
00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:09.854
Book 04 has the full library and settings,
with every external claim footnoted,

142
00:09:09.854 --> 00:09:14.816
and Book 05 covers the device compliance
that Conditional Access reads.

143
00:09:14.816 --> 00:09:15.630
Thank you.
